# Probe 302n, item b in the author's refined form (a baseline on the 301 tuple;
# CODEX-OPUS-AUTHOR-DOCUMENT-ASSIGN-MERGE-PARENS-20261107-01): test(merge(test\args; (a: int: 5))), test named tst (test
# is a word of the language), for tst (int: c 3; int: a) -- the second operand of merge a Structure with the field a; c
# has a written default, so a missing c does not hide the question; the merge's result taken as the whole structure of
# the arguments gives c 3 and a 5: 35.  Entry 7 for 35; 100 + v for another value v (191..195 a refusal: merge, call,
# implements, convert, interpreter).  Nothing here is a norm.
fn: tst (int: c 3; int: a) int
    return: c * 10 + a
end: tst
fn: inline_call () int
    ---
        int: got tst(merge(tst\args; (a: int: 5)))
        return: got
        catch: merge ()
            return: 91
        catch: call ()
            return: 92
        catch: implements ()
            return: 93
        catch: convert ()
            return: 94
        catch: interpreter ()
            return: 95
    ---
end: inline_call
fn: run () int
    int: got inline_call()
    if: got = 35
        return: 7
    ---
    return: got + 100
end: run
int: code run()
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: code; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
